  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 253 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\n\nFollowing the widespread thunder showers this morning, precipitation \nchances have generally plummeted for the rest of the day (only 10-\n30%; concentrated towards the far eastern and southern portions of \nthe area). The coverage of any overnight to Friday morning non-\nsevere storm activity, will primarily impact the eastern to \nsoutheast half of the area (mainly areas east/southeast of the Tri-\nCities). The HRRR and NAMNEST high-resolution models have recently \nbegan to show increasing potential for a broader coverage of these \nshower and weak thunderstorms during the the Thursday AM period, \nmainly across eastern Nebraska and central and eastern Kansas. \nThough precipitation totals are not expected to surpass this \nmorning's amounts, locally up to just over 1\" of rainfall may be \npossible in places through the morning. The better storm potential, \nhowever, will not come until the evening hours. \n\nA mid-to-upper level shortwave trough ejecting out from the northern \nRockies tomorrow will bring some mid-level vorticity and cold air \nadvection to the region, helping to increase instability and \nbuoyancy (2,000-3,500J/kg of MUCAPE) across the area. In addition, \nsurface pressure falls ahead of the shortwave disturbance, will help \norganize an around 1006mb surface cyclone across north central \nNebraska and central South Dakota. As this cyclone strengthens \nacross the afternoon and evening hours, a surface cold front will \nundergoing frontogenesis across central Nebraska/Kansas. This \nfeature will likely serve as the primary mechanism for evening \nthunderstorm development, likely driving a line of convection across \na majority of the area from northwest to southeast between mainly \n7PM and 1AM. Given the high instability (>2,000J/kg of CAPE), \nsteepening low-level lapse rates (up to 8-9C/km) and 25-40kts of \nbulk shear (EBWD); the potential for severe weather will remain. The \nmain concern would be for strong to damaging wind gusts as high as \n60-70MPH. Though not the primary concern, a few of the stronger \nstorms may still be able to grow tall enough to form hail stones up \nto the size of quarters. The Storm Prediction Center includes the \nfull area in a marginal risk (level 1 of 5) with most of central \nNebraska areas near and north of I-80 included in a slight risk \n(level 2 of 5). \n\nBeyond the chance for precipitation Thursday, winds out of the \nsoutheast may become breezy at times, gusting as high as 20-30MPH \nduring the afternoon hours. Highs, despite the mostly cloudy to \novercast skies will still be expected to raise as high as the upper \n80s and 90s. Following the exit of the storms Thursday night, the \nforecast remains dry through at least Monday (20-50% precipitation \nchance returns Tuesday night). Southwest U.S. ridging across the \nweekend will help fend off most precipitation chances as the jet \nstream is shoved further northward. Breezy conditions will be \nexpected to return Friday afternoon (northerly gusts as high as 25-\n35MPH) as the surface low pull east and away. lighter winds over the \nweekend will come as higher pressure resettles back into region. \nTemperatures behind the front Friday and Saturday should drop back \ndown to the 80s. Warming temperatures in response to clearing skies, \nwill warm temperatures back up to the upper 80s to mid 90s for the \nstart off next week.",
